                   SECTION 1: PROBLEM STATEMENT & MOTIVATION
===============================================================================

🔹 THE PROBLEM:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Design a QUEUE that supports all queue operations using only STACK operations.

Queue Required Operations:
• enqueue(x) - Insert element x at rear
• dequeue() - Remove and return front element
• front() / peek() - Return front element without removing
• empty() - Check if queue is empty

Available Stack Operations:
• push(x) - Add element to top
• pop() - Remove element from top
• peek() - View top element
• isEmpty() - Check if empty

🔹 THE CHALLENGE:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Queue: FIFO (First In First Out)
    enqueue(1) → enqueue(2) → enqueue(3) → dequeue() returns 1
    [1] [2] [3]
     ↑       ↑
   front   rear

Stack: LIFO (Last In First Out)
    push(1) → push(2) → push(3) → pop() returns 3
    [1]
    [2]
    [3] ← top

💡 KEY INSIGHT: Stack naturally gives us the LAST element, but Queue 
needs the FIRST element. We need to "reverse" the stack's behavior!

🔹 WHY THIS PROBLEM MATTERS:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

✅ Tests deep understanding of FIFO vs LIFO
✅ Common in technical interviews (Amazon, Microsoft, Google)
✅ Teaches data structure manipulation
✅ Shows amortized complexity analysis
✅ LeetCode #232 - Very popular problem
✅ Builds problem-solving skills
✅ Real-world: Browser history, undo/redo systems

🔹 REAL-WORLD ANALOGY:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Imagine a stack of plates (Stack):
Stack: [Plate1, Plate2, Plate3] (Plate3 on top, removes first)

To simulate a queue (oldest plate should be removed first):
We need to move plates to another stack to reverse the order!

Original Stack: [1, 2, 3] (3 on top)
Move to Helper: [3, 2, 1] (1 now on top)
Now removing from Helper gives us 1 (FIFO)! ✅

