                   SECTION 2: UNDERSTANDING THE CHALLENGE
===============================================================================

🔹 CONCEPTUAL APPROACH:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

The fundamental question: How do we make LIFO behave like FIFO?

Queue Behavior:
    enqueue(10) → enqueue(20) → enqueue(30) → dequeue() = 10
    Access: From LEFT (oldest)

Stack Behavior:
    push(10) → push(20) → push(30) → pop() = 30
    Access: From TOP (newest)

💡 SOLUTION STRATEGY:
Use TWO STACKS to reverse the order! One stack for input, one for output.

🔹 TWO POSSIBLE APPROACHES:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Approach 1: Two Stacks - Make ENQUEUE costly
    → Transfer elements during enqueue
    → Enqueue: O(n), Dequeue: O(1)
    → Simple but inefficient for frequent additions

Approach 2: Two Stacks - Make DEQUEUE costly ⭐
    → Transfer elements during dequeue (when needed)
    → Enqueue: O(1), Dequeue: Amortized O(1)
    → Efficient and preferred in interviews!

🔹 WHICH APPROACH TO USE?
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

For Interviews: ⭐ Approach 2 (Dequeue Costly - Lazy Transfer)
    ✅ Amortized O(1) for both operations
    ✅ More efficient in practice
    ✅ Shows understanding of amortized analysis
    ✅ Commonly used in production

But know both approaches to discuss trade-offs!

🔹 THE KEY INSIGHT:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

When we pop all elements from Stack1 and push to Stack2:
Stack1: [1, 2, 3] (3 on top)
    ↓ Transfer
Stack2: [3, 2, 1] (1 on top)

The ORDER IS REVERSED! Now Stack2's top has the oldest element (FIFO)! 🎯

