            SECTION 3: APPROACH 1 - TWO STACKS (ENQUEUE COSTLY)
===============================================================================

🔹 STRATEGY:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Keep elements in queue order always. Transfer during ENQUEUE.

How?
1. When enqueuing:
   - Move all elements from stack1 to stack2
   - Push new element to stack1
   - Move all elements back from stack2 to stack1
   - Result: New element at bottom, oldest at top

2. When dequeuing:
   - Simply pop from stack1 (top has oldest)

🔹 STEP-BY-STEP EXAMPLE:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Initial State:
    stack1: []
    stack2: []

Operation: enqueue(10)
Step 1: Move all from stack1 to stack2 (nothing to move)
    stack1: []
    stack2: []
Step 2: Push 10 to stack1
    stack1: [10]
    stack2: []
Step 3: Move all from stack2 to stack1 (nothing to move)
    stack1: [10]
    stack2: []

Operation: enqueue(20)
Step 1: Move all from stack1 to stack2
    stack1: []
    stack2: [10]
Step 2: Push 20 to stack1
    stack1: [20]
    stack2: [10]
Step 3: Move all from stack2 to stack1
    stack1: [20, 10]  (10 on top, 20 at bottom)
    stack2: []

Operation: enqueue(30)
Step 1: Move stack1 to stack2
    stack1: []
    stack2: [10, 20]  (20 on top)
Step 2: Push 30 to stack1
    stack1: [30]
    stack2: [10, 20]
Step 3: Move stack2 to stack1
    stack1: [30, 20, 10]  (10 on top)
    stack2: []

Operation: dequeue()
    Simply pop from stack1 → returns 10
    stack1: [30, 20]  (20 on top)

Operation: dequeue()
    Simply pop from stack1 → returns 20
    stack1: [30]

🔹 WHY THIS WORKS:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

By transferring during enqueue, we maintain queue order in stack1.
The top of stack1 always has the front of queue (oldest element).

Visual: Queue front [10, 20, 30] rear
        Stack1 [30, 20, 10] with 10 on top
        ✅ Top matches front!

