===============================================================================
         SECTION 4: APPROACH 2 - TWO STACKS (DEQUEUE COSTLY) ⭐⭐⭐
===============================================================================

🔹 STRATEGY: THE OPTIMAL SOLUTION (AMORTIZED O(1))
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Use TWO STACKS smartly:
- stack1 (input): For enqueue operations
- stack2 (output): For dequeue operations

Key Optimization: Only transfer when stack2 is EMPTY!

How?
1. When enqueuing:
   - Simply push to stack1 (O(1))

2. When dequeuing:
   - If stack2 is NOT empty: pop from stack2 (O(1))
   - If stack2 IS empty: transfer all from stack1 to stack2, then pop

🔹 THE MAGIC: LAZY TRANSFER
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

We don't transfer every time! We only transfer when stack2 becomes empty.
This means each element is transferred AT MOST ONCE, giving amortized O(1)!

🔹 DETAILED STEP-BY-STEP EXAMPLE:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Initial State:
    stack1 (input): []
    stack2 (output): []

Operation: enqueue(10)
    Simply push to stack1
    stack1: [10]
    stack2: []

Operation: enqueue(20)
    Simply push to stack1
    stack1: [10, 20]  (20 on top)
    stack2: []

Operation: enqueue(30)
    Simply push to stack1
    stack1: [10, 20, 30]  (30 on top)
    stack2: []

Operation: dequeue()
    stack2 is empty, so transfer from stack1
    
    Transfer:
        Pop 30 from stack1, push to stack2: stack2: [30]
        Pop 20 from stack1, push to stack2: stack2: [30, 20]
        Pop 10 from stack1, push to stack2: stack2: [30, 20, 10]
    
    Now stack2: [30, 20, 10] (10 on top)
    stack1: []
    
    Pop from stack2 → returns 10
    stack1: []
    stack2: [30, 20]  (20 on top)

Operation: enqueue(40)
    Simply push to stack1
    stack1: [40]
    stack2: [30, 20]

Operation: dequeue()
    stack2 is NOT empty, so directly pop from stack2
    Returns 20
    stack1: [40]
    stack2: [30]

Operation: dequeue()
    stack2 is NOT empty, so directly pop from stack2
    Returns 30
    stack1: [40]
    stack2: []

Operation: dequeue()
    stack2 IS empty, so transfer from stack1
    Transfer 40 to stack2
    stack1: []
    stack2: [40]
    Pop from stack2 → returns 40

🔹 WHY THIS IS OPTIMAL:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

✅ Enqueue is always O(1) - just push
✅ Dequeue is amortized O(1):
   - Sometimes O(1) when stack2 has elements
   - Sometimes O(n) when transferring
   - But each element transfers ONCE, so average = O(1)
✅ Space efficient - only uses two stacks
✅ Most practical solution
✅ Interview favorite!

🔹 AMORTIZED ANALYSIS PROOF:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Consider n enqueue operations followed by n dequeue operations:

Enqueue n times: n × O(1) = O(n)

Dequeue n times:
- First dequeue: Transfer n elements (O(n)) + pop (O(1))
- Next n-1 dequeues: Just pop (O(1) each)
- Total: O(n) + (n-1) × O(1) = O(2n) = O(n)

Total for 2n operations: O(n) + O(n) = O(2n)
Average per operation: O(2n) / 2n = O(1) ✅

Key: Each element is pushed once and popped once from each stack.

