    Support insertFront, insertRear, deleteFront, deleteRear
    Requires more stacks or complex logic

Variation 5: Priority Queue using Stacks
    Elements dequeued by priority
    Need additional sorting mechanism

🔹 INTERVIEW FOLLOW-UP QUESTIONS:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Q1: Why is approach 2 better than approach 1?
A1: Enqueue is O(1), and dequeue is amortized O(1). Enqueue is typically 
    more frequent, so this is more efficient overall.

Q2: What is amortized time complexity?
A2: Average time per operation over a sequence. Some ops may be O(n), but 
    averaged over many operations, it's O(1).

Q3: Can you implement queue using one stack?
A3: Yes, using recursion, but it's less efficient (O(n) for dequeue).

Q4: When would you use approach 1 over approach 2?
A4: If dequeue happens much more frequently than enqueue (rare).

Q5: What if we need both enqueue and dequeue to be worst-case O(1)?
A5: Not possible with just stacks. Need different data structure (linked list).

Q6: How many times is each element moved in approach 2?
A6: Exactly once - from stack1 to stack2. Never moved back.

Q7: Can this be thread-safe?
A7: Add synchronization to methods, use thread-safe Stack implementation.

